# Mutations and Diffs

## Overview

An Agent's routes, child Agents, connections, and Plugins are data on its
definition, and you can change them while it runs. Mutation Directives are
ordered, validated edits to that data. `Jido.Agent.mutate/2` applies them to
an Agent value and returns a `Jido.Agent.Diff` that lists what changed.

```text
{:ok, candidate, diff} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(agent, [Directive.route_add("greet.wave", Mut.Wave)])

Enum.map(diff.routes.added, & &1.path)
#=> ["greet.wave"]
```

When a handler returns the same Directives from a Turn, the new definition
commits together with the new state. This Livebook edits a greeter Agent
directly, reads the Diffs, then has the Agent add a route to itself from a
Turn.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## The Greeter

The `learn` route returns a Mutation Directive instead of changing state.
`Mut.Visits` is a Plugin you'll add later.

```elixir
alias Jido.Agent.Directive

defmodule Mut.Greet do
  use Jido.Action, name: "greet", schema: Zoi.object(%{name: Zoi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{name: name}, %{agent_state: state}), do: {:ok, %{state | last: "hello " <> name}}
end

defmodule Mut.Wave do
  use Jido.Action, name: "wave", schema: Zoi.object(%{})

  @impl true
  def run(_params, %{agent_state: state}), do: {:ok, %{state | last: "wave"}}
end

defmodule Mut.Visits do
  use Jido.Plugin

  @impl true
  def state_spec(_opts), do: {:visits, Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}

  @impl true
  def reduce(reduction, _opts), do: {:ok, reduction.plugin_state + 1}
end

defmodule Mut.Learn do
  use Jido.Action, name: "learn", schema: Zoi.object(%{path: Zoi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{path: path}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, state, [Jido.Agent.Directive.route_add(path, %{kind: :action, target: Mut.Wave})]}
  end
end

defmodule Mut.Greeter do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "greeter"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{last: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default("")}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/mutations")
    route "greet.hello", Mut.Greet, as: :hello
    route "greet.learn", Mut.Learn, as: :learn
  end
end

agent = Mut.Greeter.instantiate!(id: "greeter-1")

Enum.map(agent.routes, & &1.path)
```

The result is `["greet.hello", "greet.learn"]`.

## Add A Route

`Jido.Agent.mutate/2` takes an Agent value and a list of Mutation Directives
and returns `{:ok, candidate, diff}`.

```elixir
{:ok, waving, diff} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(agent, [Directive.route_add("greet.wave", %{kind: :action, target: Mut.Wave})])

Enum.map(waving.routes, & &1.path)
```

The result is `["greet.hello", "greet.learn", "greet.wave"]`. New routes go at
the end of the table.

The handler uses the same map shape as `Jido.Agent.define/1`. You can also
pass a module, as in `route_add("greet.wave", Mut.Wave)`, and Jido resolves
whether it's an Action or a Flow.

`mutate/2` runs nothing and leaves `agent` unchanged. It works on definitions
and instances.

## Read The Diff

```elixir
{Jido.Agent.Diff.changed?(diff, :routes), Jido.Agent.Diff.changed?(diff, :state),
 Enum.map(diff.routes.added, & &1.path), diff.routes.order}
```

The result is:

```text
{true, false, ["greet.wave"],
 %{before: ["greet.hello", "greet.learn"], after: ["greet.hello", "greet.learn", "greet.wave"]}}
```

A Diff has one entry for each collection: `routes`, `agents`, `connections`,
and `extensions` (Plugins). Each entry has `added`, `removed`, `updated` (with
`before` and `after`), and `order` on both sides. The Diff also records
changed top-level `fields`, and `state` is either `%{before: _, after: _}` or
`nil`.

`changed?/2` checks one part. `empty?/1` checks all of them. A live Agent
Server uses the Diff to decide which child Agents and Plugin runtimes to
start, stop, or restart.

## Update And Reorder

Edits apply in list order. Each edit sees the result of the one before it.

```elixir
{:ok, tuned, diff} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(waving, [
    Directive.route_update("greet.hello", %{defaults: %{name: "world"}}),
    Directive.routes_reorder(["greet.wave", "greet.hello", "greet.learn"])
  ])

{Enum.map(tuned.routes, & &1.path),
 Enum.map(diff.routes.updated, &{&1.before.defaults, &1.after.defaults})}
```

The result is `{["greet.wave", "greet.hello", "greet.learn"], [{%{}, %{name: "world"}}]}`.

| Constructor | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `route_update(path, changes)` | Merges `handler`, `as`, or `defaults` into one route. |
| `route_replace(path, handler, opts)` | Replaces the whole route. |
| `routes_reorder(paths)` | Sets the order. Must name every path exactly once. |

Order changes behavior, because the first matching route wins.

## Batch Failures

A batch applies completely or not at all. The error `path` points at the edit
that failed.

```elixir
{:error, duplicate} = Jido.Agent.mutate(agent, [Directive.route_add("greet.hello", Mut.Wave)])

{:error, missing} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(agent, [
    Directive.route_add("greet.wave", Mut.Wave),
    Directive.route_remove("nope.missing")
  ])

{{duplicate.code, duplicate.path}, {missing.code, missing.path}}
```

The result is `{{:already_exists, [:directives, 0]}, {:not_found, [:directives, 1]}}`.
The second batch's valid first edit was not applied either. After all edits,
Jido validates the complete candidate like any other Agent.

## Edits That Cancel

The Diff compares final values, not the edit history:

```elixir
{:ok, same, diff} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(agent, [
    Directive.route_add("temp.route", Mut.Wave),
    Directive.route_remove("temp.route")
  ])

{same == agent, Jido.Agent.Diff.empty?(diff)}
```

The result is `{true, true}`.

## Plugin Edits

Plugin edits are the only mutations that change state. Adding a Plugin with
an owned field adds that field with its schema default:

```elixir
{:ok, counted, diff} = Jido.Agent.mutate(agent, [Directive.plugin_add(Mut.Visits)])

{counted.state, Jido.Agent.Diff.changed?(diff, :extensions), diff.state}
```

The result is:

```text
{%{last: "", visits: 0}, true, %{before: %{last: ""}, after: %{last: "", visits: 0}}}
```

Removing that Plugin deletes its field, so Jido requires `state: :discard`:

```elixir
{:error, refused} = Jido.Agent.mutate(counted, [Directive.plugin_remove(Mut.Visits)])
{:ok, removed, _diff} = Jido.Agent.mutate(counted, [Directive.plugin_remove(Mut.Visits, state: :discard)])

{refused.code, removed.state}
```

The result is `{:plugin_state_discard_required, %{last: ""}}`.

`plugin_add/3` accepts `initial:` to set the field's starting value.
`plugin_configure/3` changes a Plugin's options. If the owned field's schema
changes, pass `migrate: {module, function, args}` to convert the old value.

## Mutate From A Turn

A handler can return Mutation Directives with its state. Jido applies them to
the candidate before commit.

```elixir
learn = Mut.Greeter.learn_signal!(%{path: "greet.wave"})
{:ok, learned, directives} = Mut.Greeter.cmd(agent, learn)

{Enum.map(learned.routes, & &1.path), Enum.map(directives, & &1.__struct__)}
```

The result is `{["greet.hello", "greet.learn", "greet.wave"], [Jido.Agent.Directive.RouteAdd]}`.

The candidate now handles `greet.wave`. The original `agent` does not:

```elixir
wave = Jido.Signal.new!("greet.wave", %{}, source: "/guides/mutations")
{:ok, waved, []} = Mut.Greeter.cmd(learned, wave)
{:error, _no_route} = Mut.Greeter.cmd(agent, wave)

waved.state
```

The result is `%{last: "wave"}`.

## Run It Live

On a live Agent Server, the new route and the state commit as one revision.

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, greeter} = Jido.start_agent(agent)

{:ok, committed, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(greeter, learn)
{:ok, after_wave, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(greeter, wave)

{Enum.map(committed.routes, & &1.path), Jido.AgentServer.status(greeter).state_version,
 after_wave.state}
```

The result is `{["greet.hello", "greet.learn", "greet.wave"], 2, %{last: "wave"}}`.
Revision 1 added the route. Revision 2 used it.

Route edits change data only. Topology and Plugin edits also change
processes: child Agents start and stop, and Plugin runtimes restart. The
Server does that after commit, from the Diff. See
[Change Live Composition](change-live-composition.md).

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(greeter)
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Directive Reference

All constructors are in `Jido.Agent.Directive`:

| Target | Constructors |
| --- | --- |
| Routes | `route_add/3`, `route_update/2`, `route_replace/3`, `route_remove/1`, `routes_reorder/1`, `routes_set/1` |
| Child Agents | `agent_add/3`, `agent_update/2`, `agent_replace/3`, `agent_remove/1` |
| Connections | `connection_add/2`, `connection_update/2`, `connection_replace/2`, `connection_remove/2` |
| Whole topology | `topology_set/1` |
| Plugins | `plugin_add/3`, `plugin_configure/3`, `plugin_remove/2` |

Routes are keyed by path, child Agents by name, and connections by
`{from, to, path}`. Add fails on an existing key. Update, replace, and remove
fail on a missing key. [Nested Agents](declare-agent-topology.livemd) and
[Connect Agent Outputs](connect-agent-outputs.livemd) cover the topology
entries.

